#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""claude_relaunch_supervisor — the BW-4994 session-continuity supervisor (external half).

Runs as a launchd tick (see the bundled .plist; StartInterval).
Each tick it decides whether a Claude Code session needs an INTERACTIVE relaunch,
and performs it by opening a real Terminal window.

TWO RELAUNCH MODES, and the axis is WOUND-DOWN vs KILLED (not context vs usage):

  * PICKUP (`claude "/pickup <id>"`) — for a session that WOUND DOWN. It wrote a
    handoff, so a fresh session reads a current snapshot and loses nothing. This
    is the context path and it works.
  * RESUME (`claude_resume <id> "<salvage prompt>"`) — for a session KILLED
    mid-flight by a usage limit. It wrote no handoff, so there is nothing for a
    fresh session to pick up: `claude_handoff` greps the transcript and returns
    the LAST handoff block in it, which for a killed session is the one its
    PREDECESSOR handed it. The fresh session then silently re-runs a stale
    mission while the dead session's real state — live agents, dirty worktrees,
    unpushed branches — is orphaned. Measured 2026-09-04: session 22e943e2 was
    relaunched into its predecessor's handoff, whose stated goal ("nine PRs open,
    not one merged") was five merges out of date, and four interrupted agents'
    work sat unharvested until a human noticed. Resuming keeps the context that
    knows what was in flight, so the session can salvage its own work.

A THIRD MODE, and the one a model-scoped limit actually wants:

  * MODEL SWITCH IN PLACE (`/model <tier>` typed into the session's own live
    window) — for a session stalled on a MODEL-SCOPED limit. Such a session is
    not dead: a usage limit never kills the process, it only makes every request
    fail, so the window, the shells and the subagents are all still there. The
    correct move is the one the client's own error message names — "switch models
    with /model" — not a relaunch. Nothing is killed, nothing forks, and in-flight
    work is never orphaned because it is never interrupted.

MODEL: the supervisor picks a tier ONLY from the order the owner declared in
`~/.claude/settings.json` (`model`, then `fallbackModel`), and only after a probe
says that tier is open. It invents no preference of its own — a hardcoded table
here once pinned every relaunch to Opus and kept relaunched sessions off Fable
even when Fable had capacity.

But it can no longer decline to choose, because THE CLIENT DOES NOT APPLY THE
FALLBACK CHAIN TO AN INTERACTIVE SESSION. `claude --help` is explicit on both
counts: --fallback-model covers a primary that is "overloaded or not available",
and "(only works with --print)". A usage limit is neither, and a terminal session
is not --print. Measured 2026-09-05: three interactive sessions exhausted the
Fable pool and took five hard stops between them with ZERO Opus turns, while
settings carried `model: opus` and `fallbackModel: ["opus"]`; at 21:46:46 this
supervisor resumed one of them onto the same exhausted tier and it re-died four
seconds later, having killed the live original to get there. So the earlier
version of this note — "the client's fallbackModel chain lands the relaunch on an
open tier" — was simply false, and every model-scoped death inherited it.

Trigger feeds, in authority order (BW-4994 ancestor-staleness rule):

  1. RELAUNCH SPECS — <root>/specs/*.json, written at wind-down
     by the handoff flow (claude_write_relaunch_spec). THE SOLE AUTHORITATIVE
     pickup-target source: a spec names the session id explicitly, so the
     stale-ancestor problem (a summarization-continued session leaves its
     ancestor's transcript greppable with an outdated handoff block) cannot arise.
  2. LIMIT-DEATH WATCHDOG — a session killed by a usage limit BEFORE it could wind
     down. Detected from the newest project transcripts: limit wording in the tail
     of a transcript that has STOPPED GROWING. Wording, classification and reset
     parsing all live in relaunch_common, grounded in the client's own renderer.
     Synthesizes a spec for the NEWEST such session only; two+ distinct candidates
     in the same tick is AMBIGUITY, which notifies the owner and relaunches nothing.

How a death becomes a wait (rewritten 2026-09-03 after a three-day dormancy):

  * A SHARED pool (session / weekly) gates every model, so it must be waited out.
    The reset instant comes from the statusline's structured `rate_limits` capture
    when one brackets the death, else from the death line's own prose.
  * A MODEL-SCOPED pool (Opus / Sonnet / Fable / usage credits) does NOT gate the
    other tiers, so there is nothing to wait for: relaunch NOW and let the
    client's own `fallbackModel` chain land on a tier that is still open. This is
    the case the Fable default made reachable.
  * A death whose reset cannot be determined is NEVER dropped. It becomes
    `wait_until: "probe"` and is polled until the pool answers. The predecessor
    logged `watchdog_no_reset_time` and did nothing — 1,643 times across three
    days, for one weekly kill it had detected correctly on the very first tick.
  * Even a KNOWN reset is only an upper bound. Anthropic resets off-cycle, so a
    waiting spec is probed periodically and launches the moment the pool reopens
    rather than sleeping to a stale predicted time.

Safety rails (all required by BW-4994):
  * kill switch — the file <root>/pause stops everything;
  * crash-loop cap — more than MAX_RELAUNCHES_PER_HOUR in the rolling hour stops
    the supervisor and notifies, instead of thrashing;
  * every action (and every refusal) is appended to log.jsonl with its trigger;
  * a relaunch happens ONLY from a spec (feed 2 works by synthesizing one), and a
    consumed spec moves to done/ so the same death cannot double-launch;
  * an indeterminate probe (network down, no binary, timeout) is never read as
    either answer — it just waits for the next tick.

Standalone-testable: `claude_relaunch_supervisor --dry-run` prints decisions
without launching anything, moving anything, or spending a probe; a bare run is
one tick (the launchd entry point).
"""

import argparse
import datetime
import glob
import json
import os
import subprocess
import sys
import time

sys.path.insert(0, os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)))
import relaunch_common as rc  # noqa: E402

HOME = os.path.expanduser("~")
# Project-neutral: an existing legacy install keeps its own directory, so
# upgrading in place never orphans a running launchd job or a pending spec.
ROOT = rc.relaunch_root()
SPEC_DIR = os.path.join(ROOT, "specs")
DONE_DIR = os.path.join(ROOT, "done")
LOG_PATH = os.path.join(ROOT, "log.jsonl")
HISTORY_PATH = os.path.join(ROOT, "relaunch-history.jsonl")
PAUSE_PATH = os.path.join(ROOT, "pause")
PROBE_STATE_PATH = os.path.join(ROOT, "probe-state.json")

PROJECTS_GLOB = os.path.join(HOME, ".claude", "projects", "*", "*.jsonl")

MAX_RELAUNCHES_PER_HOUR = 3
# A transcript that has not grown for this long is "not alive". Generous on
# purpose: a thinking model can go minutes between JSONL appends; a usage-killed
# session never appends again.
DEAD_AFTER_SECONDS = 10 * 60
# Only transcripts touched within this window are watchdog candidates at all —
# older deaths were either handled or superseded by newer sessions.
WATCHDOG_LOOKBACK_SECONDS = 36 * 3600
# How many bytes of transcript tail to scan for the limit signature.
TAIL_BYTES = 256 * 1024
# Launch this long after the stated reset so the window has actually rolled.
RESET_EPSILON_SECONDS = 3 * 60

# Probe cadence while a spec waits. A probe is ~2s and ~1,440 weighted tokens —
# 0.0006% of a 5h window, and literally zero while the pool is shut, since a
# rejected request never reaches inference. Billed dollars are not the constraint
# on a subscription (see relaunch_common's cost note), so polling can be routine.
# Tighter near the predicted reset, where the answer is most likely to change.
PROBE_INTERVAL_FAR_SECONDS = 20 * 60
PROBE_INTERVAL_NEAR_SECONDS = 5 * 60
PROBE_NEAR_WINDOW_SECONDS = 60 * 60

# How long a target session's transcript must be QUIET before its successor is
# launched. Shorter than DEAD_AFTER_SECONDS on purpose: that constant answers
# "is this session dead?" for the watchdog, where a false positive opens a
# terminal against a live session; this one answers "has it stopped working?"
# before a relaunch the session itself asked for, where the cost of waiting is
# only delay. A session whose last subagent has reported goes quiet at once.
SOURCE_QUIET_SECONDS = 5 * 60

# How long a MODEL-SCOPED stall must have run before the supervisor touches it.
# This class is detected while the session is still alive, so unlike a death the
# owner may well be sitting in front of it, one keystroke from switching tier by
# hand. Short, because the in-place switch costs the session nothing.
MODEL_STALL_GRACE_SECONDS = 3 * 60

# How long a model-scoped stall may keep running after its spec was consumed
# before the supervisor is allowed to have another go. Sized to the OBSERVABLE,
# not to caution: the switch is followed by a nudge, so a session that took it
# runs a turn within about a minute and its newest turn stops being a rejection.
# Anything past that is dormancy, which is the condition this supervisor exists to
# end — so the window is short and MAX_RELAUNCHES_PER_HOUR does the escalating,
# stopping and notifying after three tries rather than retrying forever.
# Only the live-stall path ages its dedupe instant this way: a quiet death does
# not repeat, and a spec waiting days for a weekly reset must never be re-minted
# underneath itself.
STALL_RETRY_AFTER_SECONDS = 10 * 60

# The first message a RESUMED session receives. Kept free of double quotes,
# backticks and '$' because it is interpolated into an AppleScript string that is
# itself interpolated into a shell line.
SALVAGE_PROMPT = (
    "The usage limit has reset. Before anything else, salvage the work that was "
    "interrupted when the limit hit: check every agent that was in flight -- their "
    "worktrees may hold uncommitted changes and their branches unpushed commits -- "
    "harvest whatever completed, then carry on with the plan."
)

# The nudge typed into a session that was switched IN PLACE. It must not claim a
# reset — nothing reset; the session moved tier — and it must say that nothing was
# killed, because the salvage instruction above would otherwise send a session
# hunting for orphaned work that was never orphaned.
MODEL_SWITCH_PROMPT = (
    "Your model tier hit its usage limit, so I switched this session to {tier} in "
    "place -- nothing was killed, and your shells and subagents are intact. Take "
    "stock of what landed while the turns were failing, then carry on with the plan."
)
# A phrase from that prompt distinctive enough to recognise it in the transcript,
# which is how the switch confirms the nudge was SENT rather than merely typed.
NUDGE_MARKER = "switched this session to"

# How long to let the client settle between keystroke batches, and how many times
# to re-send Enter before giving up on the nudge. Both exist because the TUI's
# paste handling makes the submit racy; see switch_model_in_place.
SWITCH_SETTLE_SECONDS = 3
SUBMIT_ATTEMPTS = 3

# The same situation, but reached by relaunch because the live window could not be
# reached. Here the process really was replaced, so salvage IS the first job.
MODEL_SWITCH_SALVAGE_PROMPT = (
    "Your model tier hit its usage limit and this session was resumed on {tier} -- "
    "the limit has NOT reset. Before anything else, salvage the work that was in "
    "flight: check every agent that was running -- their worktrees may hold "
    "uncommitted changes and their branches unpushed commits -- harvest whatever "
    "completed, then carry on with the plan."
)


def log(event, **fields):
    row = {"ts": datetime.datetime.now(datetime.timezone.utc).isoformat(),
           "event": event, **fields}
    os.makedirs(ROOT, exist_ok=True)
    with open(LOG_PATH, "a") as f:
        f.write(json.dumps(row) + "\n")


def notify(title, body):
    """A macOS notification — the supervisor's only 'surface to the owner'."""
    try:
        subprocess.run(
            ["osascript", "-e",
             f'display notification "{body}" with title "{title}"'],
            check=False, capture_output=True, timeout=10,
        )
    except Exception:
        pass  # a failed notification must never break the tick


def recent_relaunch_count():
    cutoff = time.time() - 3600
    count = 0
    if os.path.exists(HISTORY_PATH):
        with open(HISTORY_PATH) as f:
            for line in f:
                try:
                    if json.loads(line).get("epoch", 0) >= cutoff:
                        count += 1
                except json.JSONDecodeError:
                    continue
    return count


def record_relaunch(spec, mode=None):
    with open(HISTORY_PATH, "a") as f:
        f.write(json.dumps({"epoch": time.time(),
                            "session_id": spec.get("session_id"),
                            "reason": spec.get("reason"),
                            "mode": mode}) + "\n")


def load_probe_state():
    try:
        with open(PROBE_STATE_PATH) as f:
            state = json.load(f)
            return state if isinstance(state, dict) else {}
    except (OSError, json.JSONDecodeError, ValueError):
        return {}


def record_probe(session_id):
    state = load_probe_state()
    state[session_id] = time.time()
    # Keep the file from growing without bound as sessions come and go.
    cutoff = time.time() - 14 * 86400
    state = {k: v for k, v in state.items()
             if isinstance(v, (int, float)) and v >= cutoff}
    tmp = PROBE_STATE_PATH + ".tmp"
    with open(tmp, "w") as f:
        json.dump(state, f, indent=1)
    os.replace(tmp, PROBE_STATE_PATH)


def probe_is_due(session_id, due_epoch):
    last = load_probe_state().get(session_id, 0)
    if not isinstance(last, (int, float)):
        last = 0
    now = time.time()
    interval = PROBE_INTERVAL_FAR_SECONDS
    if due_epoch is not None and 0 < due_epoch - now <= PROBE_NEAR_WINDOW_SECONDS:
        interval = PROBE_INTERVAL_NEAR_SECONDS
    return now - last >= interval


def spec_created_epoch(spec):
    """The spec's created_at as epoch seconds, or None if absent/unparseable."""
    raw = spec.get("created_at")
    if not raw:
        return None
    try:
        dt = datetime.datetime.fromisoformat(raw)
    except (ValueError, TypeError):
        return None
    if dt.tzinfo is None:
        dt = dt.replace(tzinfo=datetime.timezone.utc)
    return dt.timestamp()


def already_handled(session_id, death_mtime=None):
    """Does a spec (pending or done) already cover THIS death?

    Death-aware on purpose. A RESUMED session keeps its session id, so a spec that
    handled an earlier death must not veto a later one — otherwise the first
    usage-kill would be the last one this supervisor ever acted on for that
    session, and the resume path would disable itself after a single use.

    A spec covers a death only if it was written AFTER the transcript's final
    append. If the transcript has grown since (the session resumed, ran, and died
    again), that growth is a NEW death and earns a new spec. Callers that have no
    death instant to compare against get the old identity-only behaviour.
    """
    for d in (SPEC_DIR, DONE_DIR):
        for path in glob.glob(os.path.join(d, "*.json")):
            try:
                with open(path) as f:
                    spec = json.load(f)
            except (json.JSONDecodeError, OSError):
                continue
            if spec.get("session_id") != session_id:
                continue
            if death_mtime is None:
                return True
            created = spec_created_epoch(spec)
            # An unparseable/absent created_at is treated as covering the death:
            # refusing to relaunch is the safe direction for an ambiguous spec.
            if created is None or created >= death_mtime:
                return True
    return False


def project_dir_for(transcript_path):
    """~/.claude/projects/-Users-foo-bar/x.jsonl -> /Users/foo/bar.

    The slug is lossy (a dash inside a real path segment is indistinguishable
    from a separator), which used to be handled by special-casing one known
    project. It is now resolved generically, against directories that actually
    exist — so this serves any project, and worktree namespaces too.
    """
    slug = os.path.basename(os.path.dirname(transcript_path))
    return rc.resolve_project_slug(slug)


def tail_records(path, limit=200):
    """The last records of a transcript, parsed, oldest first.

    Bounded twice — by TAIL_BYTES off the end of the file and by record count —
    because one record can be hundreds of KB and only recent history matters. The
    first line after a mid-file seek is normally a fragment; it fails to parse and
    is dropped, which is the intended behaviour rather than an error.
    """
    try:
        with open(path, "rb") as f:
            f.seek(max(0, os.path.getsize(path) - TAIL_BYTES))
            raw = f.read().decode("utf-8", errors="replace")
    except OSError:
        return []
    records = []
    for line in raw.splitlines()[-limit:]:
        line = line.strip()
        if not line:
            continue
        try:
            records.append(json.loads(line))
        except json.JSONDecodeError:
            continue
    return records


def record_text(record):
    """The text a transcript record renders as, or "" for one that renders none."""
    content = (record.get("message") or {}).get("content")
    if isinstance(content, str):
        return content
    if isinstance(content, list):
        return " ".join(block.get("text", "") for block in content
                        if isinstance(block, dict) and block.get("type") == "text")
    return ""


def record_epoch(record):
    """A record's timestamp in epoch seconds, or None if it carries none."""
    raw = record.get("timestamp")
    if not isinstance(raw, str):
        return None
    try:
        return datetime.datetime.fromisoformat(
            raw.replace("Z", "+00:00")).timestamp()
    except ValueError:
        return None


def own_turns(records):
    """The session's OWN assistant turns, oldest first — sidechains excluded.

    A subagent's turns land in the SAME transcript with ``isSidechain: true``, and
    a stalled session with a live agent keeps producing them. Counting those as
    the session's own turns would hide the stall behind its agents' progress.
    """
    return [r for r in records
            if r.get("type") == "assistant" and r.get("isSidechain") is not True]


def is_limit_rejection(record):
    """Is this record the client's own limit rejection?

    A transcript can carry limit wording without having died of one — agent
    task-notifications and quoted text embed the exact provider message
    mid-conversation (measured: the 2026-08-30 orchestrator transcript carries its
    killed AGENTS' notifications while the session itself survived). So marker
    presence is NOT a death; the death is a record the client itself marked as an
    API error (``isApiErrorMessage: true`` — the same field the statusline context
    calculation filters on) carrying limit wording.
    """
    if record.get("isApiErrorMessage") is not True:
        return False
    return rc.is_limit_death(record_text(record))


def terminal_limit_record(records):
    """The text of the session's own killing limit rejection, or None.

    "Terminal" means its LAST main-line assistant turn is a limit rejection — the
    session has had no successful turn since. Anchoring on the last turn rather
    than "a rejection somewhere in the tail" is what makes this safe to run
    against a transcript that is still GROWING, which a model-scoped stall always
    is: the session stays alive, its agents keep reporting, and every wake-up
    earns a fresh rejection, while a recovered session carries a real model on its
    newest turn. Strict on purpose: a missed relaunch degrades to the owner
    noticing; a false one opens terminals against sessions that never died.
    """
    turns = own_turns(records)
    if not turns or not is_limit_rejection(turns[-1]):
        return None
    return record_text(turns[-1])


def stall_started_epoch(records):
    """When the CURRENT unbroken run of limit rejections began, or None.

    Every wake-up during a stall earns its own rejection, so the NEWEST death is a
    moving target — deduping against it would synthesize a fresh spec every tick,
    forever. The run's FIRST rejection is stable for as long as the stall lasts,
    and a successful turn ends the run, so a genuinely new stall gets a genuinely
    new instant.
    """
    started = None
    for record in reversed(own_turns(records)):
        if not is_limit_rejection(record):
            break
        epoch = record_epoch(record)
        if epoch is not None:
            started = epoch
    return started


def last_sidechain_epoch(records):
    """When a subagent last appended to this transcript, or None if none has."""
    for record in reversed(records):
        if record.get("isSidechain") is True:
            return record_epoch(record)
    return None


def watchdog_synthesize(dry_run):
    """Feed 2: turn an un-wound-down limit death into a spec (newest session only).

    TWO SHAPES, and the difference is whether the session survived its limit:

      * a SHARED-pool death goes quiet, because every model is refused and the
        session has nothing left to do. Quiet-for-DEAD_AFTER_SECONDS is the
        detector, as it always was.
      * a MODEL-SCOPED stall stays LOUD. A usage limit never kills the process, so
        the session sits there alive with its agents still reporting, and each
        report earns another rejection. Waiting for it to go quiet means waiting
        for every agent to drain — measured 2026-09-05, session 358a1131 was
        detected only 11 minutes after its last agent finished, having stalled 20
        minutes earlier. So this class is detected on the SHAPE of the transcript
        (its last own turn is a rejection), not on silence.
    """
    now = time.time()
    candidates = []
    for path in glob.glob(PROJECTS_GLOB):
        try:
            mtime = os.path.getmtime(path)
        except OSError:
            continue
        if now - mtime > WATCHDOG_LOOKBACK_SECONDS:   # ancient
            continue
        session_id = os.path.splitext(os.path.basename(path))[0]
        records = tail_records(path)
        death_line = terminal_limit_record(records)
        if death_line is None:
            continue
        kind, limit_name = rc.classify_limit(death_line)
        quiet = now - mtime >= DEAD_AFTER_SECONDS
        # `waits_for_reset` is the authority on which pools gate every tier; an
        # unnameable limit is conservatively treated as shared by that same call.
        model_scoped = not rc.waits_for_reset(kind)
        stall_started = stall_started_epoch(records)
        if not quiet and not model_scoped:
            continue                                  # still alive and working
        if model_scoped and not quiet:
            if stall_started is None:
                continue                              # cannot date it; wait for quiet
            if now - stall_started < MODEL_STALL_GRACE_SECONDS:
                continue                              # leave room for a human
        # The instant this death is deduped against.
        if model_scoped and stall_started:
            # A live stall: the run's FIRST rejection, which holds still. The file
            # mtime does not — the stall keeps appending — and using it would mint
            # a new spec on every tick. Aged forward so that a stall STILL running
            # long after its spec was consumed (a switch typed into a window that
            # had moved on, a client that ignored the command) earns one retry per
            # window rather than lying dormant; the crash-loop cap bounds the rest.
            death_instant = max(stall_started, now - STALL_RETRY_AFTER_SECONDS)
        else:
            # A quiet death does not repeat, so its instant needs no ageing — and
            # must not get any: a spec waiting days for a weekly reset would be
            # re-minted every window, resetting the wait it is there to serve.
            death_instant = mtime
        # A spec written before this instant belongs to an EARLIER death of the
        # same (resumed) session and must not veto this one.
        if already_handled(session_id, death_mtime=death_instant):
            continue
        candidates.append({"mtime": mtime, "session_id": session_id, "path": path,
                           "death_line": death_line, "kind": kind,
                           "limit_name": limit_name, "quiet": quiet,
                           "model_scoped": model_scoped,
                           "stall_started": stall_started})

    if not candidates:
        return
    candidates.sort(key=lambda c: c["mtime"], reverse=True)

    # THE AMBIGUITY RULE GUARDS THE DESTRUCTIVE PATH, and only it. Relaunching
    # kills the original process to avoid forking it, so picking the wrong session
    # of several costs real work and BW-4994 forbids the guess. Switching a
    # session's tier in its OWN window destroys nothing and is decided per session
    # from that session's own transcript, so several at once is not a guess — it
    # is simply several sessions to switch, which is the ordinary shape of a
    # model-scoped limit (measured 2026-09-05: two stalled at once, and the old
    # rule would have refused both). A cohort member that cannot be switched in
    # place is NOT quietly downgraded into the guess this rule forbids — see
    # launch(), which refuses that degrade.
    switchable = [c for c in candidates if c["model_scoped"]]
    quiet_deaths = [c for c in candidates if not c["model_scoped"]]
    for candidate in switchable:
        write_watchdog_spec(candidate, cohort=len(switchable) > 1,
                            dry_run=dry_run)

    if not quiet_deaths:
        return
    if len({c["session_id"] for c in quiet_deaths}) > 1:
        names = ", ".join(c["session_id"][:8] for c in quiet_deaths)
        log("watchdog_ambiguous",
            candidates=[c["session_id"] for c in quiet_deaths])
        notify("Claude relaunch supervisor",
               f"Multiple limit-dead sessions ({names}) — not relaunching. "
               f"Write a spec for the right one.")
        return
    write_watchdog_spec(quiet_deaths[0], cohort=False, dry_run=dry_run)


def write_watchdog_spec(best, cohort, dry_run):
    """Turn one watchdog candidate into a pending spec."""
    mtime = best["mtime"]
    session_id = best["session_id"]
    path = best["path"]
    death_line = best["death_line"]
    kind, limit_name = best["kind"], best["limit_name"]

    # Structured epoch seconds from the statusline capture beat parsing prose;
    # prose is the fallback, and neither being available is survivable now.
    reset = rc.structured_reset(kind, mtime)
    reset_source = "statusline"
    if reset is None:
        reset = rc.parse_reset(death_line, mtime)
        reset_source = "transcript-prose" if reset else "none"

    if rc.waits_for_reset(kind):
        # A shared pool blocks every tier — the allotment is genuinely spent, so
        # there is nothing to do but wait it out. Which TIER the relaunch then
        # runs on is the client's decision, from settings' model/fallbackModel.
        if reset is not None:
            wait_until = (reset + datetime.timedelta(
                seconds=RESET_EPSILON_SECONDS)).isoformat()
        else:
            # Unknown reset is NOT a reason to do nothing. Poll until it opens.
            wait_until = "probe"
        # A watchdog death wrote no handoff, so there is nothing to /pickup:
        # resume the session itself and have it salvage its own in-flight work.
        mode = "resume"
    else:
        # Model-scoped: another tier is open right now, so there is nothing to
        # wait for — and nothing to relaunch either. The session is still ALIVE,
        # only refusing turns, so the move is the one its own error message names:
        # switch tier in its own window. `launch` degrades this to a relaunch that
        # names the tier only when that window cannot be reached.
        wait_until = "now"
        mode = "model_switch"

    spec = {
        "session_id": session_id,
        "reason": "usage-crash-watchdog",
        "mode": mode,
        "wait_until": wait_until,
        "limit_kind": kind,
        "limit_name": limit_name,
        "reset_source": reset_source,
        "project_dir": project_dir_for(path),
        "created_by": "watchdog",
        "created_at": datetime.datetime.now(datetime.timezone.utc).isoformat(),
        # Which detector fired: a quiet transcript, or the shape of a still-live
        # one. Worth keeping — they answer different questions after the fact.
        "detected_live": not best["quiet"],
        # True when this session was one of several stalled at the same instant.
        # Safe to switch in place; never safe to degrade into a relaunch.
        "cohort": cohort,
    }
    if best["stall_started"] is not None:
        spec["stall_started_at"] = datetime.datetime.fromtimestamp(
            best["stall_started"], datetime.timezone.utc).isoformat()
    log("watchdog_spec_synthesized", **spec)
    if dry_run:
        print(f"[dry-run] would synthesize spec: {json.dumps(spec)}")
        return
    os.makedirs(SPEC_DIR, exist_ok=True)
    with open(os.path.join(SPEC_DIR, f"spec-{session_id}.json"), "w") as f:
        json.dump(spec, f, indent=1)


def transcript_path_for(session_id):
    """The largest live transcript for this session id, or None."""
    matches = glob.glob(os.path.join(
        HOME, ".claude", "projects", "*", f"{session_id}.jsonl"))
    if not matches:
        return None
    try:
        return max(matches, key=lambda p: os.path.getmtime(p))
    except OSError:
        return matches[0]


def transcript_mtime(session_id):
    path = transcript_path_for(session_id)
    if path is None:
        return None
    try:
        return os.path.getmtime(path)
    except OSError:
        return None


def relaunch_mode(spec):
    """'resume' (continue the SAME session) or 'pickup' (start a fresh one).

    See the module docstring: the axis is wound-down vs killed. A watchdog spec is
    by construction a session that died before it could write a handoff, so there
    is no handoff to pick up and resuming is the only lossless move. An explicit
    `mode` in the spec always wins, so a spec can ask for either.
    """
    explicit = spec.get("mode")
    if explicit in ("resume", "pickup", "model_switch"):
        return explicit
    return "resume" if spec.get("created_by") == "watchdog" else "pickup"


def resume_blocked_reason(spec):
    """Why this session must NOT be resumed right now, or None if it is safe.

    `claude --resume` on a session that is already running starts a COPY (the
    CLI says so itself), which forks the conversation. Between synthesis and
    launch a human may well have resumed the session by hand — that is exactly
    what happened on 2026-09-04 — so liveness is re-checked at the moment of
    launch, not just when the death was detected.
    """
    session = spec["session_id"]
    path = transcript_path_for(session)
    if path is None:
        return "no transcript found to resume"
    mtime = transcript_mtime(session)
    if mtime is not None and time.time() - mtime < DEAD_AFTER_SECONDS:
        return "transcript is growing again (already resumed elsewhere)"
    return None


def source_still_working(spec):
    """Is the session this spec targets STILL PROCESSING WORK? Reason, or None.

    Applies to ALL THREE modes, because acting early is wrong in each — for
    different reasons:

      * resume — a live process means `--resume` would fork a COPY, and the
        retirement hook resolves that by KILLING the original, so anything still
        running under it dies with it.
      * pickup — a live source means two sessions working the same plan. Measured
        2026-09-04: a session at 83% context wrote a `reason=context` spec while a
        developer agent was still running. That spec carries `wait_until: "now"`,
        so the supervisor launched on the very next tick and the successor came up
        while the predecessor was still appending (23:33:59 launch, 23:34:16
        append). The handoff flow says "wait until your subagents finish", but that
        is prose addressed to the model, not a gate — so nothing enforced it.
      * model_switch — text typed at a session mid-turn is queued, not run, so a
        `/model` sent into a busy window may simply sit in its prompt buffer.

    TWO SIGNALS, because neither covers the other:

      * TRANSCRIPT GROWTH, not process liveness. A wound-down session's process
        sits alive at its prompt exactly like a usage-killed one, so pid-alive
        cannot tell "working" from "finished"; a session with a live subagent
        keeps appending, so growth does.
      * LIVE TOOL SHELLS, because growth misses them entirely. A backgrounded Bash
        command keeps its shell alive while appending NOTHING, so a session can
        look quiet for an hour with a build still running under it. This is the
        owner's requirement of 2026-09-05: let background work finish first.
    """
    session = spec["session_id"]
    entry = rc.live_session_process(session)
    if entry:
        shells = rc.running_tool_shells(entry["pid"])
        if shells:
            return (f"{len(shells)} background shell(s) under pid "
                    f"{entry['pid']}: {shells[0][:90]}")
    path = transcript_path_for(session)
    if path is None:
        return None  # nothing to race with; the mode-specific gate handles it
    mtime = transcript_mtime(session)
    if mtime is None:
        return None
    idle = time.time() - mtime
    if idle >= SOURCE_QUIET_SECONDS:
        return None

    if relaunch_mode(spec) == "model_switch":
        # A stalled session appends CONSTANTLY without doing any work: each
        # wake-up earns a fresh rejection. Reading that as "still working" would
        # hold the switch for as long as the stall lasts, which is exactly
        # backwards. Only a SIDECHAIN append — a subagent still reporting — is
        # work here, and the shell check above covers the rest.
        agent_epoch = last_sidechain_epoch(tail_records(path))
        if agent_epoch is None:
            return None
        agent_idle = time.time() - agent_epoch
        if agent_idle >= SOURCE_QUIET_SECONDS:
            return None
        return (f"subagent still reporting ({agent_idle:.0f}s ago; "
                f"needs {SOURCE_QUIET_SECONDS}s quiet)")

    return (f"source session still appending "
            f"({idle:.0f}s ago; needs {SOURCE_QUIET_SECONDS}s quiet)")


def retire(spec_path):
    """Move a consumed spec to done/, preserving any earlier spec for the session.

    Timestamped on collision because a resumed session keeps its id, so a second
    death produces a second spec with the same filename; overwriting would erase
    the record of the first.
    """
    base = os.path.basename(spec_path)
    target = os.path.join(DONE_DIR, base)
    if os.path.exists(target):
        root, ext = os.path.splitext(base)
        stamp = time.strftime("%Y%m%dT%H%M%SZ", time.gmtime())
        target = os.path.join(DONE_DIR, f"{root}-{stamp}{ext}")
    os.replace(spec_path, target)


def resume_helper_for(project):
    """The claude_resume helper to drive a resume with, or None for none found.

    It reconciles the archive against the live transcript and resolves the project
    namespace — work a relaunch should not reimplement.

    Order: the claude-tooling GOLD copy first, then the project's own. A project
    copy is a synced artifact (`sync_tooling` overwrites
    `<project>/.claude/scripts/*` from claude-tooling), so it can lag the gold
    one. A lagging copy degrades safely rather than breaking — its old arg loop
    reads the id and ignores the trailing prompt, resuming without the salvage
    instruction — but preferring the gold copy means that rarely has to happen.
    `CLAUDE_TOOLING_DIR` overrides where the gold copy lives.
    """
    tooling = os.environ.get(
        "CLAUDE_TOOLING_DIR", os.path.join(HOME, "projects", "claude-tooling"))
    candidates = [
        os.path.join(tooling, "scripts", "claude_resume"),
        os.path.join(project, ".claude", "scripts", "claude_resume"),
    ]
    for path in candidates:
        if os.access(path, os.X_OK):
            return path
    return None


def build_command(spec, project, tier=None):
    """The shell command a relaunch runs, and the mode it represents.

    --model is named ONLY when `tier` is passed, which happens only on the
    degraded model_switch path — a session stalled on an exhausted tier whose live
    window could not be reached. Resuming it without naming a tier restores the
    session's own model selection, i.e. the exhausted one, and it re-dies within
    seconds (measured 2026-09-05: relaunch at 21:46:46, rejections at 21:46:49 and
    21:46:50). For every other relaunch the tier stays the client's decision, from
    settings' model/fallbackModel — naming one here once pinned every relaunch to
    Opus.
    """
    session = spec["session_id"]
    explicit = spec.get("cmd")
    if explicit:
        return explicit, spec.get("mode") or "custom"
    mode = relaunch_mode(spec)
    if mode in ("resume", "model_switch"):
        if tier:
            prompt = MODEL_SWITCH_SALVAGE_PROMPT.format(tier=tier)
            model_arg = f" --model {tier}"
        else:
            prompt = SALVAGE_PROMPT
            model_arg = ""
        helper = resume_helper_for(project)
        if helper:
            # The helper takes leading flags before the id: `--model M <id> [prompt]`.
            return f'{helper}{model_arg} {session} \\"{prompt}\\"', "resume"
        # No helper on this machine: resume directly. Loses the archive/live
        # reconciliation, keeps the thing that matters — the same session.
        return (f'claude --resume {session}{model_arg} \\"{prompt}\\"', "resume")
    return f'claude \\"/pickup {session}\\"', "pickup"


def iterm_write(guid, text):
    """Type one line into the iTerm session with this id.

    Sessions are addressed by GUID rather than by pane coordinates — panes move
    when tabs are rearranged, ids do not — and the window is deliberately NOT
    activated: the owner may be typing somewhere else, and iTerm delivers to a
    background session perfectly well.
    """
    osa = (
        'tell application "iTerm"\n'
        '  repeat with w in windows\n'
        '    repeat with t in tabs of w\n'
        '      repeat with s in sessions of t\n'
        f'        if id of s is "{guid}" then\n'
        f'          tell s to write text "{text}"\n'
        '        end if\n'
        '      end repeat\n'
        '    end repeat\n'
        '  end repeat\n'
        'end tell'
    )
    subprocess.run(["osascript", "-e", osa], check=True, timeout=30)


def nudge_landed(session_id, since_epoch):
    """Did the nudge reach the session as a real submitted message?

    Asked of the TRANSCRIPT, not the screen: once submitted the text appears in
    the scrollback too, so the screen cannot tell "typed" from "sent" — which is
    the only distinction that matters here.
    """
    path = transcript_path_for(session_id)
    if path is None:
        return False
    for record in reversed(tail_records(path, limit=40)):
        epoch = record_epoch(record)
        if epoch is not None and epoch < since_epoch:
            break
        if record.get("type") == "user" and NUDGE_MARKER in record_text(record):
            return True
    return False


def switch_model_in_place(session_id, guid, tier):
    """Switch a live session's tier in its own window. True if the nudge landed.

    THE NUDGE CANNOT BE SENT BY BLIND TIMING, which is how this was first written
    and why it is now a handshake. `write text` appends a newline, and for a short
    line that submits — measured 2026-09-05, "/model opus" ran and the client
    answered "Set model to Opus 5". The 230-character nudge did not: it arrives as
    one burst, the TUI takes it as a PASTE, and a paste's newline is inserted as
    text rather than submitting, leaving the prompt looking finished while the
    session stays stalled. Adding a fixed 1s delay then an Enter was WORSE — the
    Enter landed mid-paste and the whole nudge was discarded, reaching neither the
    buffer nor the transcript. Both failures are timing races, so the fix is to
    stop guessing: send Enter, ask the transcript whether a message actually
    arrived, and only stop when it has.

    A switch whose nudge never lands is not a disaster: the tier still changed,
    and the session's newest own turn is still a rejection, so the watchdog sees
    it as stalled and comes back inside STALL_RETRY_AFTER_SECONDS.
    """
    started = time.time()
    iterm_write(guid, f"/model {tier}")
    time.sleep(SWITCH_SETTLE_SECONDS)     # let the client apply it first, so the
    iterm_write(guid, MODEL_SWITCH_PROMPT.format(tier=tier))   # nudge is not
    for _ in range(SUBMIT_ATTEMPTS):                           # typed into the
        time.sleep(SWITCH_SETTLE_SECONDS)                      # confirmation
        if nudge_landed(session_id, started):
            return True
        iterm_write(guid, "")             # the Enter the paste swallowed
    return nudge_landed(session_id, started)


# How long to let a relaunched client boot before typing a slash command into
# it. The client has to be at its prompt or the text lands in the terminal's
# scrollback instead, so this is deliberately generous — an extra few seconds
# costs nothing on a path that only runs after a usage limit.
BOOT_SETTLE_SECONDS = 12


# A hold is correct while the source session is still working and wrong once it
# is not. Nothing distinguishes the two from outside the process, so the hold is
# bounded by TIME instead: past this, say so rather than holding in silence.
STUCK_HOLD_SECONDS = 3600
HOLD_STATE_PATH = os.path.join(rc.ROOT, "hold-state.json")


def _hold_state():
    try:
        with open(HOLD_STATE_PATH) as fh:
            state = json.load(fh)
        return state if isinstance(state, dict) else {}
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return {}


def _save_hold_state(state):
    tmp = HOLD_STATE_PATH + ".tmp"
    try:
        with open(tmp, "w") as fh:
            json.dump(state, fh, indent=1)
        os.replace(tmp, HOLD_STATE_PATH)
    except OSError:
        pass


def warn_if_hold_is_stuck(spec, reason):
    """Notify once when a spec has been held past the point of plausible work.

    A held spec logs one line per tick and blocks nothing else, so an
    unsatisfiable hold is invisible: the session it would relaunch never comes
    back, and the log looks like the supervisor doing its job. The usual cause is
    a background shell that outlived the agent which started it — a stopped
    subagent does not take its shells with it, and a shell waiting on a condition
    that can never be true waits forever.

    Notifying does not release the hold. Whether those processes are real work is
    the owner's call, and guessing wrong either kills live work or relaunches a
    session on top of itself.
    """
    session = spec["session_id"]
    state = _hold_state()
    entry = state.get(session)
    now = time.time()
    if not entry:
        state[session] = {"first_held": now, "warned": False}
        _save_hold_state(state)
        return False
    held_for = now - entry.get("first_held", now)
    if held_for < STUCK_HOLD_SECONDS or entry.get("warned"):
        return False
    entry["warned"] = True
    _save_hold_state(state)
    hours = held_for / 3600.0
    log("hold_stuck", session_id=session, held_hours=round(hours, 1),
        reason=reason)
    notify("Claude relaunch supervisor",
           f"{session[:8]} has been held {hours:.1f}h waiting on: {reason}. "
           "Check whether those processes are still doing work.")
    return True


def launch_window(spec, tier=None):
    """Open a window running this spec's relaunch command. Returns its iTerm id.

    Returns None when the window could not be opened, or when it opened under
    Terminal.app, which has no session id to address later. A None result is not
    read as "nothing happened": the caller must assume the command may be running
    and say so rather than reporting a clean failure.
    """
    project = spec.get("project_dir") or os.getcwd()
    cmd, _ = build_command(spec, project, tier=tier)
    shell_line = f"cd {project} && {cmd}"

    if not os.path.isdir("/Applications/iTerm.app"):
        osa = (f'tell application "Terminal" to do script "{shell_line}"\n'
               f'tell application "Terminal" to activate')
        try:
            subprocess.run(["osascript", "-e", osa], check=True, timeout=30)
        except (subprocess.SubprocessError, OSError) as exc:
            log("launch_window_failed", session_id=spec.get("session_id"),
                terminal="Terminal.app", error=str(exc))
            return None
        # Terminal.app opened it, but exposes no addressable session id, so a
        # follow-up slash command cannot be typed into it.
        return None

    # `id of current session` is read INSIDE the same tell block that creates the
    # window, so the id belongs to the window just opened even if the owner
    # focuses another one a moment later.
    osa = (
        'tell application "iTerm"\n'
        '  activate\n'
        '  create window with default profile\n'
        '  tell current session of current window\n'
        f'    write text "{shell_line}"\n'
        '    return id\n'
        '  end tell\n'
        'end tell'
    )
    try:
        proc = subprocess.run(["osascript", "-e", osa], check=True,
                              capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=30)
    except (subprocess.SubprocessError, OSError) as exc:
        log("launch_window_failed", session_id=spec.get("session_id"),
            terminal="iTerm", error=str(exc))
        return None
    guid = proc.stdout.strip()
    return guid or None


def arm_remote_control(guid):
    """Type /remote-control into a freshly launched session. True if sent.

    An account switch disconnects Remote Control — it is bound to the account
    that armed it — so a session relaunched on the other login starts unreachable
    from the owner's phone unless it is re-armed. This types the command the way
    the model switch does, which is proven to submit: a short line's trailing
    newline is taken as Enter rather than as a paste.

    Whether the command was ACCEPTED is not asserted. The new session's id is not
    known here (it is minted by the client after boot), so there is no transcript
    to confirm against, and claiming success from a successful keystroke would be
    exactly the unverified assertion this codebase forbids. The caller reports it
    as attempted, and a failure is visible to the owner as a session missing from
    their phone.
    """
    if not guid:
        return False
    time.sleep(BOOT_SETTLE_SECONDS)
    try:
        iterm_write(guid, "/remote-control")
    except (subprocess.SubprocessError, OSError) as exc:
        log("remote_control_arm_failed", guid=guid, error=str(exc))
        return False
    return True


def switch_account_and_relaunch(spec, dry_run):
    """Move the machine to another account and relaunch there. True if handled.

    EVERY pool is per-account, so this answers a model-scoped limit as well as a
    shared one. It is only reached after `choose_open_tier` has found no open
    tier on the current account, which is what makes the distinction moot: a
    shared pool never had a tier to move to, and a model pool has already had
    that option and exhausted it. Preferring /model where it works is handled
    upstream by trying it first, not by refusing to switch accounts here.

    The exhausted account's limit is recorded before the switch, so the next
    escalation does not walk back into it, and so the return trip can wait for a
    known reset instead of polling blind.
    """
    kind = spec.get("limit_kind")
    session = spec["session_id"]
    leaving = rc.current_account()
    if leaving is None:
        log("account_switch_unavailable", session_id=session,
            reason="cannot read the account that is logged in")
        return False

    resets_at = spec.get("resets_at") or spec.get("reset_epoch")
    if resets_at:
        rc.note_account_limit(leaving, kind, resets_at)

    target, detail = rc.choose_open_account(kind, exclude=(leaving,))
    if target is None:
        log("no_open_account", session_id=session, limit_kind=kind,
            leaving=leaving, detail=detail)
        notify("Claude relaunch supervisor",
               f"{spec.get('limit_name') or 'That pool'} is spent on every "
               f"stored account — {session[:8]} waits for the earliest reset.")
        return False

    # A shared pool has already stopped every session on this account, so the
    # switch takes nothing from them. A model pool has not, and sessions running
    # a different tier are still working — those are not disrupted for the sake
    # of one stalled session; it waits for the reset instead. Decided BEFORE the
    # dry-run returns, so a dry run reports the decision that would really be
    # taken rather than the one it would like to take.
    others = rc.live_sessions_count()
    if others and rc.account_switch_disrupts_others(kind):
        log("account_switch_deferred", session_id=session, limit_kind=kind,
            live_sessions=others, target=target, dry_run=dry_run)
        if dry_run:
            print(f"[dry-run] would DEFER: {kind} is model-scoped and {others} "
                  f"session(s) are working on {leaving}")
            return False
        notify("Claude relaunch supervisor",
               f"{session[:8]} is stalled on "
               f"{spec.get('limit_name') or kind}, but {others} other "
               f"session(s) are working on {leaving} — not switching the "
               "account out from under them.")
        return False

    if dry_run:
        print(f"[dry-run] would switch account {leaving} -> {target} "
              f"({kind} spent) and relaunch {session[:8]} with /remote-control"
              + (f"; {others} live session(s) share this account and are "
                 "already stopped by it" if others else ""))
        return True

    ok, switch_detail = rc.switch_account(target, force=bool(others))
    if not ok:
        log("account_switch_failed", session_id=session, target=target,
            detail=switch_detail)
        notify("Claude relaunch supervisor",
               f"Could not switch to {target} for {session[:8]}: "
               f"{switch_detail}")
        return False
    log("account_switched", session_id=session, limit_kind=kind,
        leaving=leaving, target=target, detail=switch_detail)

    guid = launch_window(spec, tier=None)
    if guid is None:
        # The account DID move, so say so rather than reporting a clean failure:
        # the next launch will use the new account whether or not this window
        # opened, and the owner needs to know which login they are on.
        notify("Claude relaunch supervisor",
               f"Switched to {target} ({kind} spent on {leaving}) but could "
               f"not open a window for {session[:8]} — start it by hand.")
        return True

    armed = arm_remote_control(guid)
    record_relaunch(spec, "account_switch")
    log("relaunched", session_id=session, reason=spec.get("reason"),
        mode="account_switch", account=target, guid=guid,
        remote_control=armed)
    tail = ("Remote Control re-armed." if armed
            else "Remote Control may need /remote-control by hand.")
    notify("Claude relaunch supervisor",
           f"Switched to {target} and relaunched {session[:8]} "
           f"({spec.get('limit_name') or kind} spent on {leaving}). {tail}")
    return True


def launch(spec, dry_run):
    """Act on one due spec. True when the spec was consumed, False when held.

    False means the supervisor deliberately did nothing and the spec must stay
    pending. Today that is only "no declared tier is open", where the honest move
    is to keep waiting rather than move a session onto another shut pool.
    """
    # Every spec carries project_dir (the writer records CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR; the
    # watchdog resolves it from the transcript's slug), so the fallback is only
    # for a hand-written spec that omitted it.
    project = spec.get("project_dir") or os.getcwd()
    session = spec["session_id"]
    tier = None

    if relaunch_mode(spec) == "model_switch":
        tier, detail, unknown = rc.choose_open_tier(spec.get("limit_kind"))
        if tier is None:
            log("no_open_tier", session_id=session,
                limit_kind=spec.get("limit_kind"), detail=detail,
                indeterminate=unknown)
            if unknown:
                # A probe could not answer. That is not evidence a pool is shut,
                # so nothing is switched and nothing is said; the next tick asks
                # again.
                return False
            # Every declared tier really is spent. On a MODEL-SCOPED pool that is
            # the end of the road, but a SHARED pool (5-hour, weekly) gates every
            # model on this account and says nothing about another account, so the
            # switch moves accounts instead of tiers.
            if switch_account_and_relaunch(spec, dry_run):
                return True
            notify("Claude relaunch supervisor",
                   f"{spec.get('limit_name') or 'That tier'} is spent for "
                   f"{session[:8]} and every declared fallback is too — "
                   f"widen settings' fallbackModel, or switch by hand.")
            return False
        entry = rc.live_session_process(session)
        guid = rc.iterm_guid(entry)
        open_terminals = rc.iterm_session_ids()
        # An indeterminate terminal listing is not evidence the window is gone, so
        # it degrades to a relaunch rather than being read either way.
        if guid and open_terminals is not None and guid in open_terminals:
            if dry_run:
                print(f"[dry-run] would type '/model {tier}' into iTerm session "
                      f"{guid[:8]} for {session[:8]} (pid {entry['pid']})")
                return True
            nudged = switch_model_in_place(session, guid, tier)
            record_relaunch(spec, "model_switch")
            log("model_switched", session_id=session, tier=tier,
                limit_kind=spec.get("limit_kind"), pid=entry["pid"], guid=guid,
                nudged=nudged)
            tail = ("nothing killed." if nudged
                    else "nothing killed, but it did not pick the nudge up.")
            notify("Claude relaunch supervisor",
                   f"Switched {session[:8]} to {tier} in place — "
                   f"{spec.get('limit_name') or 'limit'} spent, {tail}")
            return True
        log("model_switch_unreachable", session_id=session, tier=tier,
            pid=(entry or {}).get("pid"), guid=guid,
            terminals_listed=(open_terminals is not None))
        if spec.get("cohort"):
            # Several sessions stalled together. Switching each in place is safe;
            # relaunching one of them is exactly the guess the ambiguity rule
            # forbids, so this one waits for its window instead of being killed.
            log("cohort_degrade_refused", session_id=session, tier=tier)
            notify("Claude relaunch supervisor",
                   f"{session[:8]} is stalled on "
                   f"{spec.get('limit_name') or 'a limit'} but its window is "
                   f"gone, and others stalled with it — switch it by hand.")
            return False

    cmd, mode = build_command(spec, project, tier=tier)
    shell_line = f"cd {project} && {cmd}"
    # Prefer iTerm2 when installed (the owner's terminal — its AppleScript API
    # opens a real window + writes the command); Terminal.app is the fallback so
    # a machine without iTerm still relaunches.
    if os.path.isdir("/Applications/iTerm.app"):
        osa = (
            'tell application "iTerm"\n'
            '  activate\n'
            '  create window with default profile\n'
            '  tell current session of current window\n'
            f'    write text "{shell_line}"\n'
            '  end tell\n'
            'end tell'
        )
    else:
        osa = (f'tell application "Terminal" to do script "{shell_line}"\n'
               f'tell application "Terminal" to activate')
    if dry_run:
        print(f"[dry-run] would launch: {shell_line}")
        return True
    subprocess.run(["osascript", "-e", osa], check=True, timeout=30)
    record_relaunch(spec, mode)
    log("relaunched", session_id=session, reason=spec.get("reason"),
        mode=mode, tier=tier, shell=shell_line)
    verb = "Resumed" if mode == "resume" else "Relaunched"
    notify("Claude relaunch supervisor",
           f"{verb} session {session[:8]} ({spec.get('reason')}).")
    return True


def should_launch_now(spec, dry_run):
    """(launch?, note) for one waiting spec.

    Three shapes of wait:
      "now"    -> launch on this tick;
      "probe"  -> reset unknown; launch when a probe says the pool answers;
      <ISO ts> -> launch at that instant, OR earlier if a probe says the pool
                  reopened ahead of the predicted time (off-cycle resets are real
                  and cost two days the last time one went unnoticed).
    """
    session_id = spec["session_id"]
    wait_until = spec.get("wait_until") or "now"
    if wait_until == "now":
        return True, "due"

    due_epoch = None
    if wait_until != "probe":
        try:
            due = datetime.datetime.fromisoformat(wait_until)
        except ValueError:
            log("spec_bad_wait_until", session_id=session_id, value=wait_until)
            return False, "unparseable wait_until"
        if due.tzinfo is None:
            due = due.replace(tzinfo=datetime.timezone.utc)
        due_epoch = due.timestamp()
        if due_epoch <= time.time():
            return True, "reset time reached"

    if not probe_is_due(session_id, due_epoch):
        log("spec_waiting", session_id=session_id, due=wait_until)
        return False, "waiting"

    # A canary must name a pool, so probing still picks a model even though the
    # relaunch itself does not. Legacy specs may carry a "model" hint; specs
    # written now do not, and the kind alone answers it for every named limit.
    model = rc.probe_model_for(spec.get("limit_kind"), spec.get("model"))
    if dry_run:
        print(f"[dry-run] would probe {model} for {session_id[:8]} "
              f"(wait_until={wait_until})")
        return False, "dry-run: probe skipped"

    record_probe(session_id)
    available, detail = rc.probe_available(model)
    log("probe", session_id=session_id, model=model, available=available,
        detail=detail, due=wait_until)
    if available is not True:
        # False = still closed; None = we do not know. Neither launches.
        return False, f"probe says {available}"

    log("probe_early_release", session_id=session_id, model=model,
        due=wait_until)
    notify("Claude relaunch supervisor",
           f"Usage available ahead of schedule — resuming {session_id[:8]}.")
    return True, "probe found the pool open"


def tick(dry_run):
    os.makedirs(SPEC_DIR, exist_ok=True)
    os.makedirs(DONE_DIR, exist_ok=True)

    if os.path.exists(PAUSE_PATH):
        log("paused")
        return 0

    count = recent_relaunch_count()
    if count >= MAX_RELAUNCHES_PER_HOUR:
        log("crash_loop_cap", recent=count)
        notify("Claude relaunch supervisor",
               f"{count} relaunches in the last hour — capped. "
               f"Investigate; touch 'pause' to silence.")
        return 1

    # Keep the logged-in account's stored credential current. A session
    # refreshes its own tokens as it runs and the refresh token ROTATES when it
    # does, so a copy captured days ago is dead on arrival — and the failure only
    # shows up later, as a relaunched session sitting at a login prompt instead
    # of working. One Keychain read and one small file write per tick buys the
    # guarantee that whatever we switch away from can be switched back to.
    if not dry_run:
        rc.refresh_account_capture()

    # Feed 2 first, so a fresh death becomes a spec this same tick.
    watchdog_synthesize(dry_run)

    for path in sorted(glob.glob(os.path.join(SPEC_DIR, "*.json"))):
        try:
            with open(path) as f:
                spec = json.load(f)
        except (json.JSONDecodeError, OSError) as exc:
            log("spec_unreadable", path=path, error=str(exc))
            continue
        if not spec.get("session_id"):
            log("spec_invalid", path=path)
            continue
        try:
            go, note = should_launch_now(spec, dry_run)
        except Exception as exc:  # a broken decision must not stall the queue
            log("decision_failed", session_id=spec.get("session_id"),
                error=str(exc))
            continue
        if not go:
            if dry_run:
                print(f"[dry-run] holding {spec['session_id'][:8]}: {note}")
            continue
        # Never launch a successor while the source session is still working.
        # The two modes diverge on what to DO about it, and the difference
        # matters: a live resume target has revived itself, so its spec is moot
        # and gets retired; a live pickup target is merely still winding down, so
        # its spec is still wanted and must be HELD, not discarded.
        mode = relaunch_mode(spec)
        working = source_still_working(spec)
        if working:
            log("launch_held", session_id=spec["session_id"], mode=mode,
                reason=working)
            if dry_run:
                print(f"[dry-run] holding {spec['session_id'][:8]}: {working}")
            else:
                warn_if_hold_is_stuck(spec, working)
            continue

        # Re-check liveness at the LAST moment for a resume: --resume on a running
        # session starts a copy, and the window between deciding and launching is
        # exactly when a human resumes the session by hand.
        if mode == "resume":
            blocked = resume_blocked_reason(spec)
            if blocked:
                log("resume_skipped", session_id=spec["session_id"],
                    reason=blocked)
                notify("Claude relaunch supervisor",
                       f"Not resuming {spec['session_id'][:8]}: {blocked}.")
                if dry_run:
                    print(f"[dry-run] would skip + retire "
                          f"{spec['session_id'][:8]}: {blocked}")
                else:
                    retire(path)
                continue
        try:
            consumed = launch(spec, dry_run)
        except Exception as exc:  # a failed launch must be visible, not fatal
            log("launch_failed", session_id=spec.get("session_id"),
                error=str(exc))
            notify("Claude relaunch supervisor",
                   f"Launch FAILED for {spec.get('session_id', '?')[:8]}: {exc}")
            continue
        # A spec the supervisor deliberately declined to act on stays pending, so
        # the next tick can try again once a tier opens.
        if consumed and not dry_run:
            retire(path)
    return 0


def main():
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__.splitlines()[0])
    parser.add_argument("--dry-run", action="store_true",
                        help="print decisions; launch nothing, move nothing, "
                             "spend no probe")
    parser.add_argument("--probe", metavar="MODEL", default=None,
                        help="run one availability probe and exit (diagnostic)")
    args = parser.parse_args()
    if args.probe:
        available, detail = rc.probe_available(args.probe)
        print(json.dumps({"model": args.probe, "available": available,
                          "detail": detail}, indent=1))
        return 0
    return tick(dry_run=args.dry_run)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
